<Base title="Matter, music & the atoms between" description="Interactive animations explain why solids resist overlap and how electronic signals become the music you hear." wing="wing-science"><section class="shell page-intro"><a href="/laboratory" class="inline-link">← Laboratory</a><p class="eyebrow topic-kicker">TWO CONNECTED EXPERIMENTS</p><h1>Feel a surface.<br/><em>Hear a moving world.</em></h1><p>Why can’t your hand pass through a table? How does an electrical signal become music? Zoom into the interactions beneath ordinary experience.</p><nav class="atlas-jumps" aria-label="Experiment sections"><a href="#matter">Why solids resist overlap ↓</a><a href="#sound">Play with electronic sound ↓</a></nav></section>
 <section id="matter" class="shell section-space"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">EXPERIMENT 01 / CONTACT</p><h2>“Empty space” is not an open doorway.</h2></div></div><p class="atlas-note">Move the surfaces together. Follow the three explanation stages, then inspect the energy curve. What changes when the separation becomes small?</p><MatterLab client:load/></section>
 <section id="sound" class="shell section-space"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">EXPERIMENT 02 / ELECTRONIC MUSIC</p><h2>The pattern travels. The air moves locally.</h2></div></div><p class="atlas-note">Track the gold molecule. Change pitch, add harmonics, and hear a short phrase. Then remove the air: does a moving speaker alone guarantee transmitted sound?</p><SoundLab client:load/></section>
 <section class="shell section-space"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Follow one note through four transformations.</h2></div><div class="compare-grid"><article class="atlas-card"><span class="eyebrow">01 / ELECTRICITY</span><h3>A signal changes over time.</h3><p>Digital samples become a changing electrical signal. In a typical moving-coil speaker, current in a coil interacts with a magnetic field, producing a force. The electrons do not leave the wire and become sound.</p></article><article class="atlas-card"><span class="eyebrow">02 / MATTER</span><h3>A solid moves neighboring air.</h3><p>The attached cone moves back and forth. Forces between the atoms in its material let it move as a structure. It compresses and expands nearby air, launching a pressure disturbance.</p></article><article class="atlas-card"><span class="eyebrow">03 / HEARING</span><h3>Mechanical motion becomes a nerve signal.</h3><p>Vibration reaches the cochlea through the eardrum and middle ear. Bending microscopic hair-cell structures opens channels that help turn mechanical motion into electrical signaling in the auditory system.</p></article><article class="atlas-card"><span class="eyebrow">04 / MUSIC</span><h3>A changing pattern becomes meaningful.</h3><p>Pitch, duration, loudness, and timbre can form melodies and rhythms. Listening also involves memory, expectation, and culture. Atomic interactions make the physical process possible; individual atoms do not contain a melody.</p></article></div></section>
 <section class="shell section-space"><KnowledgeCheck client:visible question="When sound travels from a speaker to your ear, what crosses the room?" answers={['The same air molecules fly from the speaker into the ear','A pressure disturbance transfers energy through the air','Electrons leave the wire and turn into sound particles']} correct={1} explanation="Molecules move locally as the pressure disturbance propagates. Real air also has random thermal motion. Sound is a collective mechanical wave, not an electron stream through the room."/></section>
